    "created_at": "2026-03-09T16:51:55Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-03-09T16:51:55Z",
    "body": "Looking through the change history, I found that before #7815, the spec had stated that all of the `popstate`, restore persisted state, and `hashchange` steps should be performed asynchronously within the same task (that could be the source of the comment you found). It seems that the major browser engines never implemented it this way, however, and ultimately it was decided to follow what was Gecko's behaviour at the time, which lead to the spec we have now - including the ambiguity here. Now, the timing when the `hashchange` event would fire relative to tasks queued by the `popstate` handler was implementation-defined, whereas previously the hashchange would (at least, per the spec) always run synchronously right after.\n\nAs to why `hashchange` was made async in the first place - I'm not sure. It seems that's what IE8 did, and Gecko/the spec followed it for compatibility reasons? At least, the sense I get from the discussions on https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=615061 and https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=515190.",
